(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-dermatology-quiz"]={"title":"Dermatology Quiz 1 — Physical Diagnosis 2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which glands become active during puberty and secrete pheromones?","choices":["Meibomian glands","Apocrine glands","Eccrine glands","Sebaceous glands"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Their activation at puberty is what distinguishes them from eccrine glands. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Partial thickness skin loss involving the epidermis, dermis or both defines which stage of pressure ulcer?","choices":["Stage I","Stage III","Stage IV","Stage II"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — partial thickness is what distinguishes stage II from the full thickness loss of stage III. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A lesion with a central red macule and radiating spider-like arms that blanches is called what?","choices":["A spider angioma","A cherry angioma","A telangiectasia","Purpura"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the radiating arms and blanching together identify it. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What distinguishes a keloid from a hypertrophic scar?","choices":["A keloid contains no fibrous tissue","A keloid resolves spontaneously within a year","A keloid grows beyond the original wound, while a scar does not extend beyond the injured area","A keloid is thin and white in appearance, while a hypertrophic scar is thick and pink"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — extension past the wound margin is the defining feature. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which term describes lesions that remain separate from one another?","choices":["Reticular","Discrete","Confluent","Grouped"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — discrete lesions stay distinct rather than merging. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A palpable, elevated solid mass greater than two centimetres is called what?","choices":["A nodule","A cyst","A tumor","A plaque"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — neoplasms are the example given. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which finding is described by the term rubor?","choices":["Erythema, or redness of the skin","Yellowing of the skin and sclerae","Loss of normal skin colour","A bluish discoloration of the skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — rubor and erythema are used interchangeably for redness. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which piece of equipment aids inspection when lice are suspected?","choices":["A tongue depressor","A magnifying glass","A Wood's lamp","A dermatoscope"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — nits appear as tiny white ovoid granules adherent to the hair shafts. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"The angle between the nail base and the finger measures greater than one hundred and eighty degrees, and the fingertip is rounded and bulbous. What is this finding?","choices":["Clubbing","Koilonychia","Onycholysis","Paronychia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the increased angle is the measurable feature that confirms it. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A palpable, elevated solid mass measuring less than one centimetre is called what?","choices":["A papule","A macule","A nodule","A vesicle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — nevi, warts and lichen planus are the examples given. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which gland secretes sweat in order to maintain body temperature?","choices":["The apocrine (scent) gland","The sebaceous gland","The ceruminous gland","The sudoriferous (eccrine) gland"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — thermoregulatory sweating is the eccrine gland's job. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which term refers to a tinea infection of the groin?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Tinea capitis","Tinea unguium","Tinea cruris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — cruris denotes the groin. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which component of the triple response of Lewis is produced by transudation of fluid?","choices":["The reflex flare with broadening erythema","The fading of the lesion after several minutes","The formation of a linear wheal","The initial red line"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — fluid leaving the vessels creates the raised, edematous wheal. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which skin symptoms should be asked about when taking the history?","choices":["Fever, chills and night sweats","Erythema, scaling and crusting","Pruritus, pain and paresthesia","Pruritus alone, since it is the commonest skin symptom"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — itch, pain and abnormal sensation are the three symptom categories. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which features should be inspected in the nail beds?","choices":["Capillary refill time, nail bed temperature and the presence of peripheral cyanosis","Length, cleanliness and evidence of biting","Shape, size, colour, brittleness, hemorrhages, lines and grooves, clubbing and pitting","Shape and colour only, since the remainder require specialist assessment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — eight features, each of which can point toward a systemic diagnosis. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which characteristics of the skin are assessed by inspection, palpation or both?","choices":["Colour, moisture, temperature, texture, mobility and turgor, and lesions","Colour and lesions only, since the skin examination is essentially visual","Temperature and turgor only, since colour varies too much between patients","Thickness, elasticity and capillary refill time"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — six characteristics, of which colour and lesions begin with inspection and the remainder require touch. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A soft tissue infection around the cuticle or nail fold that is painful and purulent is called what?","choices":["Onycholysis","Subungual hematoma","Nail pitting","Acute paronychia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pain and purulence distinguish the acute form from the chronic one. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which historical factors should be explored for their relationship to the appearance of skin lesions?","choices":["The patient's blood type and immunisation record","Season, travel, heat or cold, previous reactions, drugs and menses","Season and travel only, since the remainder are rarely relevant","The patient's height, weight and body mass index"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — each can be the trigger that explains the timing of an eruption. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What distinguishes terminal hair from vellus hair?","choices":["Terminal hair is pigmented while vellus hair is always white","Terminal hair is coarse and grows on the scalp and in the pubic, axillary and beard areas, while vellus hair is short and fine and covers the body","Terminal hair is the short, fine hair that covers the body, while vellus hair is the coarse hair of the scalp, axillae and beard","Terminal hair grows only after puberty while vellus hair is present only in infancy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the two hair types differ in calibre as well as location. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Where are the lesions of Kaposi's sarcoma typically distributed?","choices":["Confined to the sun-exposed areas of the face and neck","Confined to a single dermatome","Confined to the skin creases and folds","Widely disseminated on the legs, trunk, arms, neck and head"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the wide dissemination is part of what makes the diagnosis recognisable. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A purple lesion does not fade when a glass slide is pressed against it. What does this indicate?","choices":["Hemorrhage in the skin","Vascular engorgement","An allergic reaction","Inadequate peripheral circulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — blood outside the vessels cannot be displaced by pressure, which is why petechiae and purpura do not blanch. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Beyond the original four melanoma warning signs, which additional letters are included?","choices":["E for evolving or elevation, F for family history, and G for growing","E for erythema, F for friability, and G for a granular surface","E for elevation only, with no further letters","F for firmness and G for granularity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the list extends past ABCD to capture change over time and inherited risk. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"An eruption confined to a single dermatome should suggest which diagnosis?","choices":["Herpes zoster","Psoriasis","Intertrigo","Tinea capitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — dermatomal distribution is the defining feature of zoster. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Warts are caused by which organism?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Human papillomavirus","Herpes simplex virus","A dermatophyte fungus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — verrucae are small harmless tumors of the skin, grey to flesh coloured, sometimes with rough hornlike projections. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Why should the patient wear a gown for the skin examination?","choices":["It prevents contamination of the patient's clothing and keeps the examination table clean between successive patients","It standardises the appearance of the skin under artificial light","It is required whenever gloves are worn during an examination","It allows inspection of the hair, the anterior and posterior body surfaces, the palms and soles, the nails and the interdigital spaces"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a complete skin examination means the whole skin surface, including areas patients rarely expose. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which term describes lesions clustered in a dermatomal distribution?","choices":["Iris","Zosteriform","Herpetiform","Serpiginous"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — named for the pattern seen in herpes zoster. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which processes can convert a primary lesion into a secondary one?","choices":["Only the passage of time, independent of any external factor","Only secondary bacterial infection of the original lesion","Disease progression, treatment, and manipulation such as picking or scratching","Disease progression alone, since treatment does not alter lesion morphology"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the patient's own handling of a lesion is as capable of changing it as the disease itself. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A nail whose proximal portion is white and whose distal portion is dark is described as what?","choices":["Beau's lines","Splinter hemorrhages","Terry's nails","Mee's lines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the proximal white and distal dark pattern defines the finding. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A generalised or diffuse distribution most commonly suggests which category of disease?","choices":["A pressure injury","An allergic reaction","A fungal infection","A malignancy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a process delivered through the bloodstream reaches the whole skin surface. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What distinguishes a nodule from a papule?","choices":["A nodule is filled with fluid, whereas a papule is a solid elevation","A nodule is flat while a papule is elevated","A nodule is transient while a papule persists","A nodule is deeper in the dermis and measures one to two centimetres"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — depth as well as size separates the two, and the examples given are basal cell carcinoma and neurofibromatosis. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 36"}]};
